WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.042
Welcome to Hyperion.

2
00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:08.617
We are following one studio through assets, commerce, payments, escrow,
governance, and operations.

3
00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:14.124
This is an isolated local demonstration with fresh test wallets and test
assets.

4
00:00:14.342 --> 00:00:17.617
The interface and the chain results you see are real.

5
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:21.914
First, click Connect wallet and choose the studio owner.

6
00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:25.949
This message signs us in; it does not authorize a payment.

7
00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:31.050
We then create Aurora Studio, giving the team one place to manage its work.

8
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:38.098
In Settings, we choose the studio display name, enable its storefront, and set
the receiving wallet.

9
00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:42.867
All eight modules are enabled.

10
00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:52.350
We add a customer member and a team approver; workspace access and onchain
signing authority remain separate.

11
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.715
Open Assets and choose Create an asset.

12
00:01:03.248 --> 00:01:09.561
Aurora credits use the standard token format, six decimal places, and an
explicit issuance cap.

13
00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:22.383
Review transaction opens the actual plan; the owner separately approves its
signature.

14
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:27.075
Next, Issue tokens sends one thousand credits to the owner.

15
00:01:37.139 --> 00:01:41.193
Amounts are entered in base units, so six decimal places matter.

16
00:01:51.257 --> 00:01:56.450
We then read the wallet balance directly from the configured chain to verify
the result.

17
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.362
The distribution workflow starts with a campaign.

18
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.332
We choose an airdrop and a two hundred credit budget.

19
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:33.450
Creating this campaign defines its rules; it does not fund the campaign or
give anyone tokens yet.

20
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.697
We fund the campaign with its full budget, then allocate that amount to the
customer member.

21
00:02:53.983 --> 00:02:57.192
Funding and allocation are separate signed actions.

22
00:03:11.477 --> 00:03:15.850
This makes the source of the tokens and the beneficiary entitlement visible.

23
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.977
Now we switch to the customer member.

24
00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.298
Claim allocation transfers the entitled tokens to this wallet.

25
00:03:49.691 --> 00:03:56.250
The balance query confirms two hundred credits, which we will use in the
following purchase and payment workflows.

26
00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.590
Back as the owner, we create a credential collection for studio memberships.

27
00:04:11.387 --> 00:04:15.097
It has zero token decimals and an explicit item cap.

28
00:04:24.895 --> 00:04:31.017
These credentials will be issued after payment, so buying and activating a
membership stay distinct.

29
00:04:32.017 --> 00:04:34.384
Open Commerce, then Private files.

30
00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:37.934
We upload the design kit and a separate members guide.

31
00:04:38.275 --> 00:04:42.722
The application encrypts these actual files and records their digests.

32
00:04:43.063 --> 00:04:48.717
A product will refer to a specific uploaded file, rather than an empty
download placeholder.

33
00:04:49.717 --> 00:04:51.660
New product creates the offering.

34
00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:59.932
We choose the settlement asset, set a ten credit price, select the design kit,
and keep the full revenue share with the studio.

35
00:05:00.697 --> 00:05:03.950
Saving produces a draft; sales are not open yet.

36
00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.334
Set up and publish first prepares the onchain payment terms.

37
00:05:18.554 --> 00:05:25.230
After that transaction finalizes, we link the exact file to its content
address and verify the product.

38
00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:39.150
Only then does the offering become available to customers.

39
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:44.117
We also create a thirty day membership with its own private guide.

40
00:05:59.461 --> 00:06:01.457
Its price is fifteen credits.

41
00:06:16.801 --> 00:06:24.950
The membership uses the credential collection we created earlier, so the paid
order and its access credential can be verified together.

42
00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.139
Switch to the customer and open the product collection.

43
00:06:37.969 --> 00:06:41.662
Buy and review prepares the purchase for this exact product.

44
00:06:50.492 --> 00:06:56.550
After signing, Check and sync status verifies the receipt and moves the order
to its payment stage.

45
00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.665
Review payment is the separate money-moving action.

46
00:07:05.314 --> 00:07:09.293
We approve it in the test wallet and verify the finalized receipt.

47
00:07:13.942 --> 00:07:20.183
The order becomes paid, and Download content now delivers the exact design kit
that was uploaded.

48
00:07:21.183 --> 00:07:24.673
The membership follows the same purchase and payment sequence.

49
00:07:34.268 --> 00:07:37.250
Watch its result: paid, awaiting fulfillment.

50
00:07:46.846 --> 00:07:50.129
Payment alone does not activate membership access.

51
00:07:59.725 --> 00:08:02.917
The publisher still needs to issue the promised credential.

52
00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:08.119
As the owner, open Orders and memberships and choose Issue membership.

53
00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.148
This signs the credential issuance.

54
00:08:26.179 --> 00:08:33.250
We then check and sync that specific transaction, changing the order to
fulfilled with its recorded access dates.

55
00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:37.119
The customer can now download the members guide.

56
00:08:37.635 --> 00:08:43.150
This is a different file from the content purchase, and its access is checked
against the membership.

57
00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:46.750
The displayed end date makes the duration visible.

58
00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.549
Returning to the owner, open Orders and memberships and choose Review refund.

59
00:08:57.343 --> 00:09:02.324
The publisher funds this refund, returning the original gross debit to the
customer.

60
00:09:07.117 --> 00:09:12.983
We approve the transaction, wait for its receipt, and use Check and sync
status to update the order.

61
00:09:17.776 --> 00:09:20.913
Its next state is refunded pending revocation.

62
00:09:25.706 --> 00:09:30.238
Now choose Review revocation and approve the separate credential action.

63
00:09:35.031 --> 00:09:40.950
A final status check completes the membership refund and removes access to its
private guide.

64
00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:47.477
In Payments, New invoice records a specific payer, receiving wallet, asset,
and amount.

65
00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:51.663
This invoice requests twenty-five credits for a studio review.

66
00:09:52.176 --> 00:09:55.183
Creating the business record does not mark it paid.

67
00:09:56.183 --> 00:09:59.139
Open the invoice and prepare its payment request.

68
00:10:07.577 --> 00:10:11.447
Once it is finalized, bind that exact request to the invoice.

69
00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:24.717
The payer link and the recorded reference now point to a single set of payment
terms.

70
00:10:25.717 --> 00:10:29.398
The named payer opens the invoice and chooses Review and pay.

71
00:10:39.863 --> 00:10:43.393
After wallet approval, we verify the linked payment receipt.

72
00:10:53.858 --> 00:11:00.950
The invoice changes to paid only after the amount, parties, asset, and
finalized chain result all match.

73
00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:06.953
The bridge page explains the source burn, attestation, and destination mint.

74
00:11:07.454 --> 00:11:11.485
We can inspect both Solana-to-Base and Base-to-Solana directions.

75
00:11:11.986 --> 00:11:21.083
This local instance has no connected Circle environment, so transfer actions
remain unavailable rather than showing a simulated success.

76
00:11:22.083 --> 00:11:26.425
For escrow, the customer becomes the buyer and the studio is the merchant.

77
00:11:54.483 --> 00:12:03.783
Create a draft order sets one milestone, funding by milestone, the original
settlement asset, and explicit funding and refund response windows.

78
00:12:04.783 --> 00:12:09.881
We configure one asset in this milestone and set the agreed principal to one
credit.

79
00:12:30.285 --> 00:12:31.747
These are draft terms.

80
00:12:52.151 --> 00:12:59.083
The current terms version is included with each operation so an outdated
agreement cannot be changed accidentally.

81
00:13:00.083 --> 00:13:01.826
Start with Set arbitrators.

82
00:13:06.707 --> 00:13:13.287
We enter separate primary and backup addresses, with one-hour response
windows, and approve these terms first.

83
00:13:18.167 --> 00:13:22.883
Next, choose Set delivery terms for the same order and its current terms
version.

84
00:13:27.763 --> 00:13:31.678
There is one milestone, with a delivery deadline three hours ahead.

85
00:13:36.559 --> 00:13:41.614
Its review window and revision window are each one hour, and the revision
limit is two.

86
00:13:46.495 --> 00:13:49.216
Review the complete transaction before signing.

87
00:13:54.097 --> 00:13:59.717
These settings record the responsibilities and deadlines that both parties
will later accept.

88
00:14:00.717 --> 00:14:02.745
Sealing follows a checked sequence.

89
00:14:11.552 --> 00:14:15.190
First, choose Begin sealing terms for the current order version.

90
00:14:23.997 --> 00:14:32.072
After that result is verified, choose Verify milestone for sealing and check
milestone zero, the only milestone in this order.

91
00:14:40.878 --> 00:14:44.941
Next, open Read chain state and request the terms digest.

92
00:14:53.747 --> 00:14:56.608
This hash identifies the configured agreement.

93
00:15:05.414 --> 00:15:10.545
Return to Seal reviewed terms and enter that exact digest with the current
version.

94
00:15:19.351 --> 00:15:23.350
The sealed order is now ready for the merchant to review and accept.

95
00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:27.010
Switch to the studio owner acting as merchant.

96
00:15:36.488 --> 00:15:39.976
Accept sealed order includes the same reviewed terms hash.

97
00:15:49.454 --> 00:15:55.650
The two parties have now accepted the same agreement; funding and delivery
still happen in later steps.

98
00:15:56.650 --> 00:16:01.161
The buyer prepares funding and creates the milestone vault for Aurora credits.

99
00:16:23.032 --> 00:16:26.860
These setup actions establish where the agreed asset will be held.

100
00:16:48.731 --> 00:16:52.783
The order is not treated as funded just because the vault exists.

101
00:16:53.783 --> 00:16:57.015
We will fund the one-credit milestone in two transfers.

102
00:17:06.404 --> 00:17:11.919
First, enter four hundred thousand base units, equal to forty percent of one
credit.

103
00:17:21.307 --> 00:17:26.586
The minimum receipt and maximum debit are explicit, and we review and sign the
transfer.

104
00:17:35.974 --> 00:17:38.771
The milestone still needs its remaining principal.

105
00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:52.426
Next, repeat the funding action with six hundred thousand base units.

106
00:18:01.814 --> 00:18:05.995
After both transfers finalize, choose Confirm milestone funding.

107
00:18:15.383 --> 00:18:21.750
This final check verifies the full agreed balance in the vault before the
merchant can deliver the work.

108
00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:27.751
The merchant submits the first delivery with its evidence address and file
digest.

109
00:18:40.003 --> 00:18:42.235
The hash commits to the delivered version.

110
00:18:54.487 --> 00:18:57.817
The buyer can now review it under the agreed review window.

111
00:18:58.817 --> 00:19:02.541
The buyer requests a revision against delivery version zero.

112
00:19:13.269 --> 00:19:17.034
The request records the digest of the requested changes.

113
00:19:27.762 --> 00:19:33.383
This stays inside the revision limit and response window already accepted by
both parties.

114
00:19:34.383 --> 00:19:38.595
Back as the merchant, we submit the revised file and its new digest.

115
00:19:50.853 --> 00:19:53.601
The expected previous revision is explicit.

116
00:20:05.859 --> 00:20:10.883
This creates the next delivery version instead of silently replacing the first
handoff.

117
00:20:11.883 --> 00:20:13.902
The buyer approves revision one.

118
00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.432
Approval records acceptance of this delivery.

119
00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:44.083
It authorizes settlement under the agreed terms, while the actual payment is
still a separate action.

120
00:20:45.083 --> 00:20:47.601
The merchant now settles the approved asset.

121
00:21:02.274 --> 00:21:06.870
This executes the actual transfer, including the configured protocol fee.

122
00:21:21.543 --> 00:21:26.683
Reading the order afterward exposes the released amount, fee, and milestone
state.

123
00:21:27.683 --> 00:21:29.712
Closure is another checked sequence.

124
00:21:38.419 --> 00:21:44.339
The buyer first chooses Prepare order closure, then Verify milestone closure
for milestone zero.

125
00:21:53.046 --> 00:21:58.048
Once those checks finish, Close completed order records the completed
lifecycle.

126
00:22:06.755 --> 00:22:11.692
Next, open the empty-vault workflow and choose Verify and retain archive.

127
00:22:20.399 --> 00:22:27.630
Hyperion reads and retains the complete closed-order evidence, displaying its
hash when verification succeeds.

128
00:22:36.338 --> 00:22:39.686
This recording ends the order with its archive preserved.

129
00:22:48.393 --> 00:22:53.683
Reclaiming rent or cleaning up its remaining chain accounts is a separate
later action.

130
00:22:54.683 --> 00:22:57.897
Treasury uses a separate onchain approval policy.

131
00:23:10.263 --> 00:23:17.793
We create a Squads team with the owner and approver, require both votes, and
set no execution delay for this demonstration.

132
00:23:30.159 --> 00:23:34.617
The browser generates the additional creation signer for this transaction.

133
00:23:35.617 --> 00:23:38.669
First, issue one hundred credits to the treasury vault.

134
00:23:43.604 --> 00:23:46.964
The recipient is the vault address created for this team.

135
00:23:51.898 --> 00:23:57.454
After that issuance is verified, return to Treasury and choose Propose a
treasury payment.

136
00:24:02.389 --> 00:24:10.452
We enter the organization, the studio owner as recipient, the same asset
configuration, and the full one hundred credit amount.

137
00:24:15.386 --> 00:24:17.331
Review and sign the proposal.

138
00:24:22.265 --> 00:24:28.217
Its payment payload is now fixed, while the funds remain governed by the two-
person approval policy.

139
00:24:29.217 --> 00:24:33.919
The owner votes to approve the proposal using its recorded payload hash.

140
00:24:39.054 --> 00:24:42.021
One vote is still below the two-person threshold.

141
00:24:47.155 --> 00:24:51.817
The transaction history records this decision separately from execution.

142
00:24:52.817 --> 00:24:59.715
The team approver signs in, reviews the same proposal index and payload hash,
and adds the second approval.

143
00:25:19.334 --> 00:25:25.317
This is the second required onchain vote, not an administrator changing a
database flag.

144
00:25:26.317 --> 00:25:29.977
With both approvals present, the owner executes the proposal.

145
00:25:44.775 --> 00:25:47.389
The approved treasury payment can now move.

146
00:26:02.187 --> 00:26:08.917
The Realms workflow is also available here for community governance, with its
own proposal and voting rules.

147
00:26:09.917 --> 00:26:15.271
For the liquidity chapter, we create a second local test asset and issue its
pool budget.

148
00:26:33.781 --> 00:26:36.927
These quote tokens are test assets, not dollars.

149
00:26:55.437 --> 00:26:59.383
Together with Aurora credits, they form the pair for the following pool.

150
00:27:00.383 --> 00:27:04.213
Open Trading and liquidity, then choose the DAMM pool workflow.

151
00:27:11.636 --> 00:27:16.318
We select a supported configuration and supply eighty units of each asset.

152
00:27:23.741 --> 00:27:28.583
The initial token ratio and both debits are explicit before wallet approval.

153
00:27:29.583 --> 00:27:32.849
First, choose Create a liquidity position for the new pool.

154
00:27:35.436 --> 00:27:39.617
Review and sign to establish the separate position and its ownership.

155
00:27:42.203 --> 00:27:45.988
Then choose Add DAMM liquidity and enter that position address.

156
00:27:48.575 --> 00:27:52.130
We set a maximum deposit of ten units for each token.

157
00:27:54.716 --> 00:27:59.707
The pool's current ratio determines the actual amounts used within those
limits.

158
00:28:02.294 --> 00:28:10.817
After approval and confirmation, the new position has its own recorded
liquidity, separate from the position created with the original pool.

159
00:28:11.817 --> 00:28:15.199
Read chain state shows the positions owned by this wallet.

160
00:28:19.794 --> 00:28:23.420
We select the separate position and review Claim DAMM fees.

161
00:28:28.014 --> 00:28:32.536
This is a fresh pool, so the demonstration assumes no earned trading fees.

162
00:28:37.131 --> 00:28:42.377
Next, choose Remove DAMM liquidity for the liquidity amount added in the
previous step.

163
00:28:46.972 --> 00:28:50.790
Both minimum outputs are entered explicitly in base units.

164
00:28:55.384 --> 00:28:58.595
Review the returned assets and approve the transaction.

165
00:29:03.189 --> 00:29:11.850
Finally, read the positions again to inspect the result; the pool's original
position remains separate from the position we just exited.

166
00:29:12.850 --> 00:29:19.729
The same interface exposes bonding-curve launches and graduation, as well as
DLMM positions.

167
00:29:20.209 --> 00:29:23.891
Jupiter swaps require their configured external provider.

168
00:29:24.371 --> 00:29:31.550
We show these separate workflows and their availability without presenting an
unexecuted swap as a completed trade.

169
00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:38.553
Staking and lending exposes Jito deposits and the separate exit, cooldown, and
claim steps.

170
00:29:39.062 --> 00:29:43.061
Kamino exposes supply, withdrawal, borrowing, and repayment.

171
00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:51.750
These providers are not connected in this local instance, so this chapter
demonstrates their forms and limits, not funded positions.

172
00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:55.857
Activity brings the recorded operations together.

173
00:29:56.363 --> 00:30:04.704
Search by a business reference and filter to finalized results, then open a
transaction to inspect its signer, state, and signature.

174
00:30:05.210 --> 00:30:08.250
This connects a business action to its chain evidence.

175
00:30:09.250 --> 00:30:12.321
Operations includes background receipt checks.

176
00:30:12.807 --> 00:30:17.756
We select an existing transaction and schedule a check of that original
result.

177
00:30:18.241 --> 00:30:23.617
This task reconciles the saved signature; it does not sign or send another
payment.

178
00:30:24.617 --> 00:30:30.013
Connections distinguishes configured adapters from services that are still
unavailable.

179
00:30:30.316 --> 00:30:34.508
Project Two B remains a reserved integration, as requested.

180
00:30:34.811 --> 00:30:45.317
The callback page accepts a real HTTPS receiver; this recording does not
register an invented endpoint or send events to an unrelated service.

181
00:30:46.317 --> 00:30:53.389
The ledger records finalized account movements in exact base units, including
applicable network fees and rent.

182
00:30:53.782 --> 00:30:57.081
It does not combine different assets into a profit figure.

183
00:30:57.474 --> 00:31:02.050
Export activity downloads the operations record for further review.

184
00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:06.847
We return to the overview and open the workspace notifications.

185
00:31:07.158 --> 00:31:14.125
The studio now has actual asset, commerce, payment, escrow, treasury, and
liquidity activity to inspect.

186
00:31:14.436 --> 00:31:22.583
The unconnected bridge, staking, lending, and Project Two B paths remain
visibly separate from those completed local workflows.
